[32m+[m[32m            <div id="incident-management" class="content-section">[m
[32m+[m[32m                <h2 class="content-title">SECURITY INCIDENT MANAGEMENT POLICY</h2>[m
[32m+[m[32m                <div class="policy-text">[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">SECURITY INCIDENT MANAGEMENT POLICY</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>This policy provides guidance, procedures, and actions to be taken in case of security or personal breach involving any data processing system of the University and/or personal data under its control and custody.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">OBJECTIVE</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>This policy is promulgated to:</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Ensure that the University, its offices, personnel, and students react appropriately to any actual or suspected security incidents relating to the data processing systems and personal data within the custody of the University.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Establish a data breach response team and define its roles and responsibilities.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Implement security measures to prevent or minimize the occurrence of a personal data breach and assure the timely discovery of a security incident.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Establish an incident response procedure to ensure the availability, integrity, and confidentiality of the personal data being processed through the University’s data processing system.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Establish procedures for mitigating possible harm and negative consequences in the event of data breach.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">SCOPE</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>For the purpose of this policy, the following terms are defined, as follows:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.1. “Personal Data” refers to personal information, sensitive personal information, and privileged information as defined by the Data Privacy Act of 2012;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.2. “Sensitive Personal Information” refers to personal information:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <p class="indent">a. About an individual’s race, ethnic origin, marital status, age, color, and religious, philosophical or political affiliations;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <p class="indent">b. About an individual’s health, education, genetic or sexual life of a person, or to any proceeding for any offense committed or alleged to have been committed by such person, the disposal of such proceedings, or the sentence of any court in such proceedings;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <p class="indent">c. Issued by government agencies peculiar to an individual which includes, but not limited to, social security numbers, previous or current health records, licenses or its denials, suspension or revocation, and tax returns, and</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <p class="indent">d. Specifically established by an executive order or an act of Congress to be kept classified</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                        [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.3. “Data Processing System” refers to the structure and procedure by which personal data is collected and processed in an information and communications system, or any other relevant filing system. It includes the purpose and intended output of the processing (National Privacy Commission, op.cit.)</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.4. “Personal Data Breach” refers to a breach of security leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to, personal data transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed. A personal data breach may be in the nature of:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. An availability breach resulting from loss, accidental or unlawful destruction of personal data;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Integrity breach resulting from alteration of personal data; and/or</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. A confidentiality breach resulting from the unauthorized disclosure of or access to personal data.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.5. “Data Processor” refers to the NEU personnel who process Personal Data pertaining to a Data Subject;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.6. “University” refers to New Era University and all its branches.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.7. “Data Subject” refers to an individual whose personal data is processed.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.8. “Personnel” refers to all members of the faculty, also called as the academic personnel, both to the teaching and non-teaching personnel, working in the New Era University Main Campus in New Era, Quezon City and in its branches in Lipa City, Batangas; City of San Fernando, Pampanga; General Santos City, South Cotabato; and Pinugay, Baras, Rizal.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.9. “Security Incident Response Team (SIRT)” refers to the personnel that would be the first responders to any and all data breach incidents. The team is also tasked to provide regular updates to the affected University personnel as to the ongoing measures to address the security incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.10. “Compliance Officer for Privacy (COP)” refers to the personnel assigned to each department of the University to oversee compliance of data privacy within the department.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.11. “Security Incident” refers to an event or occurrence that affects or tends to affect data protection, or may compromise the availability, integrity, and confidentiality of personal data. It shall include incidents that would result to a personal data breach, if not for safeguards that have been put in place. The term “Security Incident” includes “Personal Data Breach.”</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>A Security Incident includes, but is not restricted to, the following:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>The loss or theft of data or information.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>The transfer of data or information to those who are not entitled to receive that information.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Attempts (either failed or successful) to gain unauthorised access to data or information storage or a computer system.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Changes to information or data or system hardware, firmware, or software characteristics without the NEU's knowledge, instruction, or consent.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Unwanted disruption or denial of service to a system.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>The unauthorised use of a system for the processing or storage of data by any person.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.12. “Incident Report” refers to a document that provides a detailed account of a suspected security incident which could be a basis for initial assessment of the incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.13. “Risk” refers to the potential of an incident to result in harm or danger to a Data Subject or organization.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.14. “Vulnerability” refers to a weakness of a data processing system that makes it susceptible to threats and other attacks.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.15. “Threat” refers to a potential cause of an unwanted incident, which may result in harm or danger to a Data Subject, system, or organization.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.16. “DPA refers to Republic Act No. 10173, otherwise known as the Data Privacy Act of 2012.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.17. “IRR refers to the Implementing Rules and Regulations of Republic Act No. 10173, otherwise known as the Data Privacy Act of 2012.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>3.18. “NPC” refers to the National Privacy Commission of the Philippines as created by the Data Privacy Act of 2012.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">SECURITY INCIDENT RESPONSE TEAM (SIRT) AND COMPLIANCE OFFICER FOR PRIVACY (COP)</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>The NEU Security Incident Response Team (SIRT) shall be responsible for investigation of a suspected security incident and reporting to the University President and Board of Trustees.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>The COP of each department shall serve as the main point of contact for all reports of a suspected security incident coming from their assigned department.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">COMPOSITION OF SECURITY INCIDENT RESPONSE TEAM (SIRT)</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Headed by the Director of the SIRT and assisted by the Data Protection Officer, the team shall have two (2) other permanent members recommended by the Director of the SIRT and approved by the University President. Where the member of the Team is the data processor involved in a reported incident, the University president will recommend a competent alternative. Additional members shall be appointed as necessity arises and depending on the recommendation of the Director of the SIRT.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>The following offices and individuals shall perform their respective functions and responsibilities:</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>6.1. CSD</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. Responsible for the daily operation and maintenance of the University’s I.T. facilities.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Responsible for immediately reporting possible security incidents that need further investigation by the SIRT.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. Cooperate and allow the SIRT and DPO to regularly check the operations of the CSD to comply with the former’s obligation in monitoring security breaches and vulnerability of computer networks.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. Submit inventory of user access and access level control of employees as to the assigned accounts for the use of the data processing system of the University e.g. institutional email, NEU Automate, and existing google forms</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">e. Secure necessary logs in maintaining the infrastructure of the University.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>6.2. COP</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. Responsible for conducting data audits within the department. This should involve identifying:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">i. The categories of personal data processed</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">ii. The locations where it is stored e.g. physical filing cabinets, hard disks, and cloud drives.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iii. Inflows or sources of personal information e.g. google or web forms, physical forms, email, and phone calls.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iv. Outflows or third parties with whom the personal data is shared e.g. mail carriers, advertisers, cloud storage providers, and third-party online platforms.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Responsible for checking that all physical and electronic records are secured, tracked, and/or monitored.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. Records and secures all reports and documents related to suspected security incidents.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. Review and recommend, along with the head of the department, information security measures and the acquisition of necessary equipment to implement such information security measures to the University President, SIRT, and/or DPO.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">e. Assist in the implementation of information security measures within the department.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">f. Submit the Incident Report to the SIRT and DPO in accordance with this policy.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>6.3. SIRT</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. Investigate and assess suspected security incidents in coordination with all concerned units and offices of the University.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Recommend remedial measures to be performed concerning a suspected security incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. Accomplish a Security Incident Report, as prescribed by the Board of Trustees.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. Recommend permanent and additional members of SIRT to the University President.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>6.4. SIRT Head and DPO</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. Serve as the main point of contact for all reports of a suspected security incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Act as custodian of all reports and documents generated or prepared in relation to suspected security incidents. Document all the remedial actions taken.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. Review and revise this Policy in accordance with the Philippine Data Privacy Act of 2012 and other laws, guidelines, and standards applicable to the protection of Data Processing Systems and Personal Data.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. Report to the University President, recommend and implement approved remedial measures to be performed in relation to a suspected security incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">e. Notify the NPC and/or affected data subjects when required by the Data Privacy Act of 2012.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>6.5. University President</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. Approve, reject, or otherwise take action on the findings or recommendations of the SIRT and DPO.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Appoint the permanent members of the SIRT.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. Appoint the alternate of any permanent member of the SIRT, when necessary.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. Approve, reject, or otherwise comment on proposed revisions to this Policy.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">e. Approve identified and authorised staff that should have access to the affected systems during the incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">PROCEDURE FOR REPORTING SECURITY INCIDENT</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Security incidents as well as events and weaknesses of the data processing system need to be reported at the earliest possible stage as they need to be assessed by the Data Protection Officer and the Head of the SIRT. The Data Protection Officer enables the Security Incident Response Team (SIRT) to identify when a series of events or weaknesses have escalated to become an incident. The SIRT needs to gain as much information as possible from the data subjects or personnel to identify if an incident is occurring.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Examples of the security events, weaknesses, and common forms of Security Incidents have been provided below:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Security events can include:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Uncontrolled system changes.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Access violations – e.g. password sharing.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Breaches of physical security.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Loss of ID badge/s</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Missing correspondence</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Exposure of Uncollected print-outs</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Misplaced or missing media</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Loss of mobile phones and portable devices</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Systems being hacked or manipulated.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Non-compliance with policies.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Security weaknesses can include:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Inadequate firewall or antivirus protection.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>System malfunctions or overloads.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Malfunctions of software applications.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Human errors.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>The most common Security Incidents are listed below. It should be noted that this list is not exhaustive.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p><strong>Malicious</strong></p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Giving information to someone who should not have access to it - verbally, in writing, or electronically.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Computer infected by a Virus or other malware.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Sending a sensitive e-mail to 'all staff' by mistake.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Receiving unsolicited mail of an offensive nature.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Receiving unsolicited mail that requires you to enter personal data.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Finding data that has been changed by an unauthorized person.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Receiving and forwarding chain letters – including virus warnings, scam warnings, and other emails that encourage the recipient to forward to others.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Unknown people ask for information that could gain them access to the University data (e.g. a password or details of a third party).</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p><strong>Misuse</strong></p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Use of unapproved or unlicensed software on the University’s equipment.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Accessing a computer database using someone else's authorization (e.g. someone else's user ID and password).</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Writing down your password and leaving it on display / somewhere easy to find.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Printing or copying confidential information and not storing it correctly or confidentially.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p><strong>Theft / Loss</strong></p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Theft/loss of a hard copy file.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Theft/loss of any of the University’s computer equipment.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.1. Notification or Report to the Data Protection Officer and the Security Incident Response Team (SIRT)</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">Security Incident Report shall be submitted or coordinated through:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">• The Data Protection Officer (DPO) and/or</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">• Head of the Security Incident Response Team (SIRT)</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">• Email addresses: dpo-notification@neu.edu.ph</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">• Contact Numbers: 02 8981 4221 local 3859</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">The following information should be provided:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 80px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Details of the incident (if known)</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Date and Time of Incident</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Number of Persons Affected</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Name of office processing the information</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Contact name and number of the person reporting the incident.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>The type of data, information, or equipment involved.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Whether the loss of the data puts any person or other data at risk.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Location of the incident.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Inventory numbers of any equipment affected.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Location of data or equipment affected.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.2. The SIRT shall investigate and assess the reported suspected security incidents in coordination with the concerned unit or office.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.3. The SIRT shall inform immediately, or within one (1) hour from the discovery of the security incident, to the University President for appropriate action. A Security Incident Report shall be accomplished within twenty-four (24) hours and submit the same to the University President.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.4. The SIRT shall evaluate the Security Incident Report submitted to them. The Head of the SIRT will determine if additional members or experts are necessary to investigate the reported incident. If deemed necessary, the Head of the SIRT shall recommend the designation of additional members to the University President.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.5. The SIRT shall conduct its investigation based on the Security Incident Report submitted and shall perform the following tasks:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. Collate the following information:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">i. The nature and circumstances of the security incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">ii. Data processing systems involved.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iii. Data Processor and other persons responsible or involved, designation and their contact details.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Conduct clarificatory questions to the Data Processor.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. Require additional submissions of documents, reports, or information from the Data Processor.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. Request for a meeting with the Data Processor and other concerned offices or individuals in the University.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">e. Perform actions to obtain the necessary information related to the reported Security Incident.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">f. Consult with the DPO and Head of the SIRT before performing major actions that may need permission from the University President and/or Board of Trustees.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">g. Complete its investigation within forty-eight (48) hours after the endorsement of the Security incident Report for investigation. Any request for extension of investigation and submission of initial assessment shall be relayed to the University President, DPO, and Head of the SIRT.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.6. The results of the investigation and initial recommendation (SIRT Security Incident Report) shall be signed by all members of the SIRT and submitted to the University President, copy furnished to the DPO.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.7. The University President, DPO and Head of the SIRT shall then make a final recommendation to the Board of Trustees for appropriate approval and action.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>7.8. All documents submitted related to the Security Incident shall be consolidated and kept by the DPO and Head of the SIRT.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">SECURITY MEASURES</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>8.1. The DPO and Head of the SIRT, with the approval of the University President, will conduct a debriefing session with the COPs, Data Processor and concern individuals to implement remedial or preventive measures. The DPO may also conduct an orientation regarding data privacy and compliance with the Data Privacy Act of 2012.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>8.2. The DPO and Head of the SIRT may recommend the conduct of Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) on the data processing system involved in the security incident or the office concern.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>8.3. The University President shall issue a corresponding memorandum to implement the new security measures as recommended by the DPO and the Head of the SIRT.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>8.4. The SIRT shall conduct the following:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; color: #444; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 40px; margin-bottom: 20px;">[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Regular monitoring for security breaches and vulnerability scanning of computer networks;</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Change user and administrative access credentials;</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Conduct backup of data as deemed necessary. General or scheduled back-up shall be implemented by CSD;</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Shut down or disconnect the affected devices from the internet or intranet;</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Conduct inventory and review of access control, equipment and assets, and secure log files as deemed necessary. General inventory shall be done by CSD and reported to the SIRT and DPO;</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Restore, repair or rebuild the system or application that was compromised.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                        <li>Review and approve the Assets Inventory Report conducted by the CSD.</li>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    </ul>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>8.5. The Computer Service Department (CSD) shall remain to be responsible for the daily operation and maintenance of the University’s I.T. facilities. They shall immediately report to the SIRT in case there is a belief or possibility that a security incident might arise and need further investigation by the SIRT. They will cooperate and allow the SIRT and DPO to regularly check the operations of the CSD to comply with the former’s obligation to monitor security breaches and vulnerability of computer networks.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>8.6. The Acceptable Use Policy of the University, including but not limited to the Information Security Policy, shall govern the use of computing facilities and network infrastructure in the University.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">CONFIDENTIALITY</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>All information related to reported security incidents shall be kept confidential by all concerned Parties. A corresponding Non-Disclosure Agreement prescribed by the DPO shall be explained to the concerned parties and signed by them before they assume their functions under this Policy. Any public pronouncements involving any security incidents must be upon the approval of the DPO, University President, and/or Board of Trustees.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">DATA BREACH NOTIFICATION OF THE NPC AND DATA SUBJECTS</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>The DPO shall assess and recommend whether the Personal Data Breach should be reported to the National Privacy Commission (NPC) as required under the Data Privacy Act of 2012.</p>[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>The following shall be reported to NPC:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. The personal data involves sensitive personal information or any other information that may be used to enable identity fraud;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. Other information includes, but is not limited to, the following:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">i. Data about the financial or economic situation of the data subject;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">ii. Usernames, passwords, and other login data;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iii. Biometric data;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iv. Copies of identification documents, licenses, or unique identifiers like Philhealth, SSS, GSIS, TIN number; or</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">v. Other similar information, which may be made the basis of decisions concerning the data subject, including the grant of rights or benefits.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. There is reason to believe that the information may have been acquired by an unauthorized person; and</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">d. The personal information controller believes that the data breach is likely to give rise to a real risk of serious harm to the affected data subject.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>When there is doubt as to whether notification is necessary, consider factors:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. The likelihood of harm or negative consequences on the affected data subjects;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. How notification, particularly of the data subjects, could reduce the risks arising from the personal data breach reasonably believed to have occurred; and</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. If the data involves:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">i. Information that would likely affect national security, public safety, public order, or public health;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">ii. At least one hundred (100) individuals;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iii. Information required by all applicable laws or rules to be confidential; or</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent" style="padding-left: 60px;">iv. Personal data of vulnerable groups.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Aside from notifying the NPC, the University, through the DPO, SIRT, and University President, shall also notify the affected Data Subjects within <strong>seventy-two (72) hours</strong> upon knowledge of or the reasonable belief by the University that a Personal Data Breach has occurred.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[41m                    [m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Generally, there shall be no delay in notification, except to the extent necessary to determine the following:</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">a. The scope of the breach;</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">b. To prevent further disclosures; or</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p class="indent">c. To restore reasonable integrity to the information and communications system.</p>[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Delay is not allowed if the breach involves at least one hundred (100) data subjects, or the disclosure of sensitive, personal information will harm or adversely affect the data subject except when the University is granted additional time by the NPC to comply.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">LEARNING FROM INFORMATION SECURITY INCIDENTS</h3>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <p>Security Incidents shall be recorded and reviewed to improve the response process of the University. The information collated shall be considered in reviewing and recommending the revision of the University’s Data Privacy Policy.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m                    <h3 class="section-heading">POLICY GOVERNANCE</h3>[m
